# Negative control (the forge half): the devbox image with the forge binaries removed.
#
# The sibling of `Dockerfile.no-forge`, for the other half of the image tier.
# `tests/image/test_devbox_image.py` runs its assertions against a
# separately built image, and reading them tells you almost nothing about
# whether they can fail — the lesson of ISSUE-263, where four Group A tests
# passed against an image with no forge binaries in it at all.
#
# **Three forge controls, not one, and that was measured rather than assumed.** The
# file asserts three separable things: that the two images ship the same forge
# versions, that the vendored wrapper in the image is byte-identical to
# `src/istota/forge_cli.py`, and that `gh` and `glab` resolve on PATH to the
# wrapper rather than to a real binary. This image breaks the first, and the
# second only in the weak sense that the file is gone rather than different.
# It does not touch the third at all: `/usr/local/bin/gh` is a *copy* of the
# wrapper (`docker/devbox/Dockerfile:156`), not a symlink into this directory,
# so it survives the `rm` and four of the forge assertions stay green.
# Hence `Dockerfile.devbox-stale-wrapper` and
# `Dockerfile.devbox-real-binary-on-path`.
#
# Test-owned, deliberately, for the same reason as its sibling: the
# alternative was a build arg in the production recipe whose only purpose is
# producing a broken artifact. Nothing in a real build reads this file.
#
# Usage — it takes the real devbox image as its base rather than rebuilding:
#
#   docker build -f docker/test/Dockerfile.devbox-no-forge \
#       --build-arg BASE=<the tag tests/image/conftest.py built> \
#       -t istota-test/devbox-no-forge:local docker/test
#
# Fed to the tier through ISTOTA_DEVBOX_IMAGE_TAG, which
# `tests/image/conftest.py:363` already honours.
ARG BASE
FROM ${BASE}

# The devbox image ends on `USER dev` (docker/devbox/Dockerfile:199) and the
# forge directory is root-owned, so the control's own edit needs root. Dropped
# back to `dev` at the end, so the image the tier receives differs from the
# product in exactly one way rather than two.
USER root

# The directory rather than the two binaries: an image that ships a third
# binary later should lose that one too, or the control stops matching what
# the product does. This also removes the vendored wrapper that lives
# alongside them, which is why the byte-identity assertion goes red here for
# the wrong reason — it cannot read the file at all. That assertion's real
# control is the stale-wrapper image.
RUN rm -rf /usr/local/lib/istota_forge

# What goes with the two binaries: the vendored wrapper
# (istota_forge_cli.py) and seed-forge-policy, which live in the same
# directory. What does not: /etc/istota-forge/policy.json, which is written
# elsewhere and survives, and the four wrapper copies at /usr/local/bin, which
# are copies rather than symlinks. /etc/gitconfig still names the credential
# helper. Stated precisely because this file's whole value is being accurate
# about what a control does and does not touch.

USER dev
